          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Flex Seal on a basement wall</strong> is a scale problem, not a quality problem. Around a pipe, along a window frame, or over a cracked sump basin it does exactly what it says. Spread across hundreds of square feet of damp block with water pressure behind it, it is being asked to be a waterproofing system, which it was never designed or tested to be.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed">
            Diagnose first: one entry point or a whole surface? A patch or a system? If it is a patch, reach for the liquid rubber and get on with your evening. If it is a system, strip the wall back to bare masonry and put two proper coats on it — and if water is arriving after every storm, neither product is your answer, because the fix is outside the wall.
          </p>
